.\" Man page for AmberEdit. CMake configures it into
.\" build/generated/amberedit.1, filling in @PROJECT_VERSION@ and the paths this
.\" build installs to: the version goes on standing only in
.\" project(AmberEdit VERSION ...), and a page installed under another prefix
.\" names that prefix rather than /usr/share.
.\"
.\" The date in .TH is the page's own and is written by hand. It says when the
.\" page was last changed, which is not when a release was cut.
.TH AMBEREDIT 1 2026-09-23 "AmberEdit @PROJECT_VERSION@" "AmberEdit Manual"
.SH NAME
amberedit \- FidoNet mail editor for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B amberedit
[\fB\-c\fR \fIconfig\fR]
[\fB\-o\fR \fIline\fR]...
[\fB\-\-compile\fR]
.br
.B amberedit \-\-setup
.br
.B amberedit
\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-V\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B amberedit
reads, writes and changes FidoNet mail in Squish, JAM and Fido
.I *.msg
message bases. It is a reader and an editor, not a mailer and not a tosser:
what it writes goes out when a tosser carries it.
.PP
The areas are the ones your system already has. AmberEdit reads them from the
tosser's own configuration \(em a fidoconfig, an
.I areas.bbs
or a
.I squish.cfg
\(em rather than keeping a list of its own; areas may also be written into the
config in
.B area ... endarea
blocks.
.PP
Four screens stand one behind the other: the area list, the message list, the
reader and the editor. Messages are read and written both in UTF\-8 and in the
single\-byte charsets FidoNet runs on \(em CP866, CP437, KOI8\-R, LATIN\-1 \(em
converted per area where an area group says so. A UTF\-8 terminal locale is
recommended.
.PP
Nodelists, pointlists and echolists the config names are compiled at every
start, whenever the files on disk have changed; one that is missing or will not
read is a warning on the way past and never stops the program.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIpath\fR
Read this config. Without it one is looked for in the three places under
.B CONFIGURATION
below.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-option " " \fIline\fR
One whole line of the config \(em the same spelling, the same quoting \(em
standing in place of whatever the file said about that setting for this run.
May be given as many times as there are settings to change.
.IP
A key the config may repeat, like
.B aka
or
.BR nodelist ,
loses the whole of what the file said rather than being added to, so the option
states that list entire. The
.B area ... endarea
and
.B group ... endgroup
blocks are not written this way, a block being several lines; a group goes on
laying its settings over what
.B \-o
left. A value that is not a config line at all is refused before a config has
been looked for.
.TP
.B \-\-setup
Run the setup wizard: seven steps \(em who you are, which tosser config you
keep and where, the charset your mail is read in and the one it is written in,
the charset the config file and everything it names is written in, a nodelist if
you have one, and where the config goes \(em and it writes the config. It takes
neither
.B \-c
nor
.BR \-o ,
writing a config rather than reading one, and it refuses to run where there is
a config already, naming the one it found.
.TP
.B \-\-compile
Compile the nodelists and echolists even though they look unchanged, then start
as usual. They are compiled at every start when they have changed, so this is
for the case where the compiled file is to be built again regardless.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the program, the system it was built for and the version, as in
.BR AmberEdit/@AMBEREDIT_SYSTEM@\ @PROJECT_VERSION@ ,
and exit. It is the same string the tearline of every message AmberEdit writes
carries.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Without
.B \-c
the config is looked for in
.BR $AMBEREDIT_CONFIG ,
.IR ./amberedit.cfg ,
then
.IR ~/.ambereditrc ,
and the first that is there is the one read. Where none of the three is,
AmberEdit says so and stops:
.B \-\-setup
writes one, or
.I amberedit.cfg.example
is copied and edited.
.PP
That sample config is the reference for every setting \(em what it takes, what
it defaults to, and what it means. It is installed as documentation; see
.B FILES
for where your package put it.
.PP
The config is UTF\-8 unless
.B config_charset
says otherwise, and whatever it says holds for every file the config names as
well: the tosser config, the message template, the themes, and the files a
setting may keep its values in.
.PP
A
.B group ... endgroup
block states settings for the echo areas its
.B member
patterns match \(em the charsets, the origin, the template, the twits and the
rest of what is per\-area rather than per\-config.
.SH KEYS
.B F1
opens the key list of whichever screen is up, each key with what it does beside
it. It is the layout read back rather than a fixed page, so a rebound key is the
key the box shows.
.B Ctrl\-Q
quits from anywhere.
.PP
Every key that runs a command can be moved: the
.B keys
setting names a layout of your own and
.B keys_mode
says whether it is read on top of the standard one or in place of it.
.I amberkeys.cfg.example
is a layout to copy.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
AmberEdit ran, or
.B \-\-setup
wrote a config.
.TP
.B 1
It could not start: no config found, a config, keys file or theme that will not
read, a tosser config or message base that will not open, a key bound to an
external utility no
.B extern_util\fIN\fR
line names \(em or, under
.BR \-\-setup ,
a config already there, or the user leaving without writing one.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong: an unknown option, an option whose value is
missing, an
.B \-o
line that is not a config line,
.B \-\-setup
written together with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-o ,
or no terminal for the wizard to ask in.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AMBEREDIT_CONFIG
The config to read, looked at before
.I ./amberedit.cfg
and
.IR ~/.ambereditrc ,
and only where
.B \-c
was not given.
.TP
.BR LANGUAGE ", " LC_ALL ", " LC_MESSAGES ", " LANG
The language the interface is drawn in, gettext's own order of preference:
.B LANGUAGE
first and as a colon\-separated list, then the first of the other three that is
set. Nothing goes in the config for this \(em
.B LANG=ru_RU.UTF\-8 amberedit
is Russian, as it is for every other program on the system. A language whose
catalog is installed but whose locale the system has not generated is a warning
and an English interface, never a failure.
.TP
.B TERM
The terminal, and so what it can draw. Set it to
.B xterm\-256color
where colors come out wrong.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell the user's own shell command opens. Where it is unset, the shell the
password file gives for the user is opened, and
.I /bin/sh
where that says nothing either. An external editor or utility is not run through
a shell at all: its line is the program and its arguments, and the program is
looked for on
.BR $PATH .
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Where the working directory goes when the config's
.B temp_dir
names none: an
.I amberedit
directory of the user's own is made under it, or under
.I /tmp
where it is unset. Unpacking a zipped nodelist or echolist and handing a message
to an external editor happen there.
.TP
.B HOME
What a
.RB \(dq ~/ \(dq
in the config stands for, and where
.I ~/.ambereditrc
is looked for.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ./amberedit.cfg ", " ~/.ambereditrc
Where a config is looked for, in that order, after
.BR $AMBEREDIT_CONFIG .
.TP
.I @CMAKE_INSTALL_FULL_DATADIR@/amberedit/default.tpl
The message template the sample config's
.B template
line names: the header lines and the quote of a reply, in GoldED\-style tokens.
A template of one's own is a copy of this, named by that setting.
.TP
.I @CMAKE_INSTALL_FULL_DATADIR@/amberedit/themes/*.cfg
The themes, named by the config's
.B theme
setting.
.I black.cfg
is the built\-in palette written out \(em what AmberEdit draws with when the
config names no theme, and the file to copy and edit.
.TP
.I @CMAKE_INSTALL_FULL_LOCALEDIR@/*/LC_MESSAGES/amberedit.mo
The message catalogs the interface is drawn from.
.PP
The rest of what AmberEdit reads and writes is where your config says it is,
and nowhere by default: the message bases under the tosser config, the compiled
nodelist and echolist that
.B nodelist_db
and
.B echolist_db
name, and the log that
.B error_log
names \(em which is written to only when something has gone wrong, and is
neither rotated nor emptied. Read marks are not a file of ours at all: they are
kept in the message base itself, under the user number
.B lastread_user
names.
.SH EXAMPLES
A first config, then the reader:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
amberedit \-\-setup
amberedit
.EE
.RE
.PP
A light theme and a narrower quote margin, for this run only:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
amberedit \-o "theme themes/white.cfg" \-o "quote_margin 72"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Another config, with the nodelists built again on the way in:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
amberedit \-c ~/ftn/etc/amberedit.cfg \-\-compile
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR amberedit.cfg.example ,
the reference for every setting, and
.IR amberkeys.cfg.example ,
a keyboard layout to copy \(em both installed as documentation, with
.IR README.md ,
.IR KEYS.md ,
.IR KEYS_REBINDING.md ,
.I INSTALL.md
and
.IR PuTTY-truecolor.md .
.PP
AmberEdit is at
.UR https://github.com/jegornet/amberedit
GitHub
.UE .
.SH AUTHOR
Yegor Gluhov <git@jegor.net>
